```

### Token Impact Analysis

| Scenario | Tools Loaded | Estimated Tokens |
|---|---|---|
| Initial load (navigator + common) | 4 tools | ~1,500 |
| After loading tickets domain | ~20 tools | ~8,000 |
| After loading tickets + KB | ~30 tools | ~12,000 |
| All domains loaded | ~60 tools | ~25,000 |
| Naive approach (all at once) | 200+ tools | ~80,000+ |

## Lazy Loading Implementation

Each domain is implemented as a self-contained module that exports a `register(server)` function. The module is not imported until `tdx_navigate` requests it.

```
src/
├── index.ts                  # Entry point, transport setup
├── server.ts                 # MCP server instance, navigator registration
├── config.ts                 # Environment/config management
├── auth/
│   └── token-manager.ts      # JWT lifecycle, auto-refresh
├── http/
│   └── client.ts             # HTTP client with rate-limit handling
├── domains/
│   ├── registry.ts           # Domain registry, lazy loader
│   ├── tickets/
│   │   ├── index.ts          # register() export
│   │   ├── tools.ts          # Tool definitions
│   │   ├── handlers.ts       # Tool handlers
│   │   └── types.ts          # TypeScript interfaces
│   ├── knowledge-base/
│   │   ├── index.ts
│   │   ├── tools.ts
│   │   ├── handlers.ts
│   │   └── types.ts
│   ├── people/
│   │   ├── index.ts
│   │   ├── tools.ts
│   │   ├── handlers.ts
│   │   └── types.ts
│   ├── assets/
│   │   ├── index.ts
│   │   ├── tools.ts
│   │   ├── handlers.ts
│   │   └── types.ts
│   └── ... (additional domains)
├── middleware/
│   ├── rate-limiter.ts       # Per-endpoint rate limit tracking
│   └── error-handler.ts     # Standardized error responses
└── transport/
    ├── stdio.ts              # stdio transport setup
    └── http.ts               # HTTP/SSE streamable transport setup
```

## Dual Transport Support

The server must support both transport protocols:

**stdio** — For local execution with Claude Desktop, Claude Code, and development/testing. The server reads JSON-RPC messages from stdin and writes responses to stdout. This is the default mode.

**HTTP (Streamable HTTP + SSE)** — For remote/shared deployments. The server exposes an HTTP endpoint that supports the MCP Streamable HTTP transport (server-sent events for server-to-client messages, POST for client-to-server messages). This enables Cowork and multi-user scenarios where the MCP server runs as a shared service.

Transport selection is controlled by environment variable or CLI flag:

```
TDX_MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio     # default
TDX_MCP_TRANSPORT=http      # HTTP mode
TDX_MCP_HTTP_PORT=3000      # HTTP port (default 3000)
TDX_MCP_HTTP_HOST=0.0.0.0   # HTTP bind address
```

## Authentication & Token Management

The `TokenManager` class handles the JWT lifecycle:

1. On first API call, authenticate using `POST /api/auth/loginadmin` with BEID + WebServicesKey
2. Cache the JWT token in memory
3. Decode the JWT to extract the `exp` claim
4. Set a refresh timer for 1 hour before expiry (tokens last 24 hours, so refresh at 23 hours)
5. On 401 responses, immediately re-authenticate and retry the failed request once
6. Never persist tokens to disk

## Rate Limit Management

The `RateLimiter` middleware:

1. Tracks rate limit state per-endpoint using the `X-RateLimit-*` response headers
2. Before each request, checks if the endpoint has remaining capacity
3. If remaining = 0, calculates wait time from `X-RateLimit-Reset` and queues the request
4. Implements an exponential backoff with jitter for 429 responses
5. Enforces a minimum 5-second wait floor per TDX documentation recommendation
6. Exposes current rate limit state through the `tdx_status` tool

---

# Configuration

All configuration is provided through environment variables with sensible defaults. No config files.

| Variable | Required | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `TDX_BASE_URL` | Yes | — | TDX instance base URL (e.g., `https://yourschool.teamdynamix.com`) |
| `TDX_BEID` | Yes | — | Administrative service account BEID (Guid) |
| `TDX_WEB_SERVICES_KEY` | Yes | — | Administrative service account WebServicesKey (Guid) |
| `TDX_TICKETING_APP_ID` | No | — | Default ticketing application ID |
| `TDX_ASSETS_APP_ID` | No | — | Default assets/CI application ID |
| `TDX_KB_APP_ID` | No | — | Default knowledge base application ID |
| `TDX_MCP_TRANSPORT` | No | `stdio` | Transport protocol: `stdio` or `http` |
| `TDX_MCP_HTTP_PORT` | No | `3000` | HTTP transport listen port |
| `TDX_MCP_HTTP_HOST` | No | `0.0.0.0` | HTTP transport bind address |
| `TDX_LOG_LEVEL` | No | `info` | Logging level: `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error` |
| `TDX_PRELOAD_DOMAINS` | No | `tickets` | Comma-separated list of domains to pre-load at startup |
| `TDX_RATE_LIMIT_BUFFER` | No | `5` | Reserve this many rate limit slots as headroom |

