# Docker & Distribution

## Docker Image

The Docker image will be published to GitHub Container Registry at `ghcr.io/chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server`.

### Dockerfile Requirements

* Multi-stage build: build stage (Node.js 20 + TypeScript compilation) → production stage (Node.js 20 slim)
* Non-root user execution
* Health check endpoint (for HTTP transport mode)
* Signal handling for graceful shutdown (SIGTERM, SIGINT)
* Labels following OCI image spec (org.opencontainers.image.source, .description, .licenses)
* Support for both stdio and HTTP transport modes via environment variables

### Image Tags

* `latest` — latest stable release
* `x.y.z` — semver release tags
* `sha-<short>` — commit SHA tags for development
* `edge` — latest commit on main branch

### Compose Example

```yaml
services:
  tdx-mcp:
    image: ghcr.io/chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server:latest
    environment:
      TDX_BASE_URL: https://yourschool.teamdynamix.com
      TDX_BEID: ${TDX_BEID}
      TDX_WEB_SERVICES_KEY: ${TDX_WEB_SERVICES_KEY}
      TDX_TICKETING_APP_ID: "431"
      TDX_MCP_TRANSPORT: http
      TDX_MCP_HTTP_PORT: "3000"
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"
```

## npm Package

The npm package will be published to GitHub Packages at `@chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server`.

### Package Artifacts

* Compiled JavaScript (ESM) in `dist/`
* TypeScript type declarations in `dist/`
* CLI entry point for `npx @chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server`
* `bin` field in `package.json` pointing to the CLI entry

### Installation Methods

```bash
# Via npx (stdio mode, for Claude Desktop config)
npx @chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server

# Global install
npm install -g @chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server

# Docker (HTTP mode)
docker run -e TDX_BASE_URL=... -e TDX_BEID=... -e TDX_WEB_SERVICES_KEY=... \
  ghcr.io/chattstate/teamdynamix-mcp-server:latest
```

---

# CI/CD Pipeline

## GitHub Actions Workflows

### Build & Test (`ci.yml`)

Triggered on push to main and pull requests.

1. Checkout code
2. Setup Node.js 20
3. Install dependencies (`npm ci`)
4. Lint (`eslint`)
5. Type check (`tsc --noEmit`)
6. Run unit tests (`vitest`)
7. Run integration tests (if TDX_BASE_URL secret is configured)
8. Build (`tsc`)
9. Build Docker image (no push)

### Release (`release.yml`)

Triggered on semver tags (`v*`).

1. All steps from CI
2. Build and push Docker image to ghcr.io with appropriate tags
3. Publish npm package to GitHub Packages
4. Create GitHub Release with changelog

### Nightly (`nightly.yml`)

Triggered on cron schedule.

1. All steps from CI
2. Build and push Docker image with `edge` tag

---

# Testing Strategy

## Unit Tests

Every domain module will have corresponding test files using Vitest:

* Mock the HTTP client to test tool handlers without hitting the real API
* Test parameter validation for all tool schemas
* Test rate limit tracking logic
* Test token refresh lifecycle
* Test decision tree navigation and domain loading

## Integration Tests

Optional tests that run against a real TDX sandbox (gated behind environment variable):

* Authentication flow (admin login, token refresh)
* Ticket CRUD lifecycle
* KB article CRUD lifecycle
* Search operations
* Rate limit header parsing

## MCP Protocol Tests

Test the MCP protocol layer:

* Tool listing (verify only navigator tools on startup)
* Tool registration after navigate call
* `notifications/tools/list_changed` emission
* stdio transport message framing
* HTTP transport SSE stream management

---

# Security Considerations

* The BEID and WebServicesKey are sensitive credentials — they must never be logged, included in error messages, or exposed through the `tdx_status` tool
* JWT tokens are cached in memory only and never persisted to disk
* The HTTP transport should be deployed behind a reverse proxy with TLS in production
* The Docker image runs as a non-root user
* All user-supplied input to API calls is validated against the tool schema before forwarding to TDX
* The server does not support arbitrary API passthrough — only defined tools can be invoked

---

# Phased Delivery

## Phase 1 — Core (Weeks 1-4)

* Project scaffolding (TypeScript, ESM, Vitest, ESLint)
* MCP server framework with dual transport (stdio + HTTP)
* Authentication (admin login, token lifecycle)
* Rate limit middleware
* Decision tree navigator (`tdx_navigate`, `tdx_status`)
* Tickets domain (all tools)
* Knowledge Base domain (all tools)
* People domain (search, get, lookup)
* Docker image build + ghcr.io publish
* npm package build + GitHub Packages publish
* CI/CD pipeline
* Unit tests (>80% coverage)

## Phase 2 — Extended (Weeks 5-8)

* Assets/CMDB domain (all tools)
* People domain (full CRUD, group/role management)
* Projects domain (search, get, tasks)
* Time domain (time entries)
* Reference data caching (statuses, priorities, types — refresh every 4 hours)
* Integration test suite
* Documentation site (README, API reference, deployment guide)

## Phase 3 — Advanced (Weeks 9-12)

* Reports domain
* Service Catalog domain
* Webhook management tools
* MCP Resources support (expose ticket/article data as MCP resources for context)
* MCP Prompts support (pre-built prompts for common workflows like "triage this ticket")
* Admin domain (locations, accounts, attributes)
* Performance optimization and caching

